Arguments in UiPath

Arguments are used to pass data from one workflow (automation) to another workflow. They are similar
to variables because they store data, but their main purpose is to exchange data between workflows
----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
Diff B/w Variables and Arguments:

Variables	Arguments
1. Variables are used to store data within a workflow.      	  1. Arguments are used to pass data between workflows.
2. Variables pass data between activities.                    	  2. Arguments pass data between automations/workflows.
3. Variables have only Name, Data Type, Scope, and Default Value.	3. Arguments have Name, Direction, Data Type, and Default Value.
4. Created in the Variables panel.	                              4. Created in the Arguments panel.
5. Used for calculations and temporary data storage.            	5. Used for communication between workflows.
6. Have a scope property.	                                          6. Have a direction property.
---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
Example

Suppose you have two workflows:

Main.xaml

Calls CalculateSalary.xaml
Sends Employee ID using an In Argument

CalculateSalary.xaml

Receives Employee ID
Calculates salary
Sends Salary back using an Out Argument
Types of Arguments

Arguments support the same data types as variables:

String - Stores text values
Boolean - Stores True/False values
Int32/Number - Stores numeric values
Object - Stores any type of data
Array - Stores a collection of values
DataTable - Stores tabular data
Generic Value - Stores different types of values dynamically
DateTime - Stores date and time values
Argument Directions
Direction	Purpose
In	Receives data into a workflow
Out	Sends data out of a workflow
In/Out	Receives and sends data
Property	Used for library components
